# ── 4.1 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
rep("""Three perspectives on ophthalmic–systemic risk — systemic-route database severity, evidence-graded rules and prescribing clinicians — assign grades differently, and this study measures that difference on overlapping pair sets. Absorption-aware assessment grades three quarters of the registry-matched pairs lower than the database and a further 7.0% higher, so the two differ for more than four pairs in five, and the downgrades are dominated by low topical systemic absorption. Evidence-graded rules and clinical judgment also diverge: the two ophthalmologists agreed closely with each other (kappa 0.840) yet rated most cases one tier below the assigned grades (Section 3.8). Because the panel was not independent and the cases were built around the system's outputs, this is a perspective comparison rather than a validation. Three observations make a perspective difference more plausible than an error: clinically serious events from ophthalmic–systemic DDI are rare, so expert judgment rationally gravitates toward lower tiers; the interactions clinicians recall are dominated by non-selective beta-blockers, so systematic coverage is most valuable where recall is weakest; and reports of serious adverse events are sparse, although pharmacovigilance analyses of ophthalmic agents do identify systemic signals [36,40]. High alert-override rates [29] further suggest that a tool sitting one tier above clinical intuition will be treated as noise unless its justifications are transparent, which is what the traceable decision paths provide. The results are therefore consistent with the absence of a shared grading standard for this route rather than with error in any one tool.""",
    """Three perspectives on ophthalmic–systemic risk — systemic-route database severity, evidence-graded rules and prescribing clinicians — assign grades differently, and this study measures that difference on overlapping pair sets. Absorption-aware assessment grades three quarters of the registry-matched pairs lower than the database and a further 7.0% higher, so the two differ for more than four pairs in five. Evidence-graded rules and clinical judgment also diverge: the two ophthalmologists agreed closely with each other (kappa 0.840) yet rated most cases one tier below the assigned grades (Section 3.8). Because the panel was not independent and the cases were built around the system's outputs, this is a perspective comparison rather than a validation. Three observations make a perspective difference more plausible than an error: clinically serious events are rare, so expert judgment rationally gravitates toward lower tiers; the interactions clinicians recall are dominated by non-selective beta-blockers, so systematic coverage matters most where recall is weakest; and reports of serious adverse events are sparse, although pharmacovigilance analyses do identify systemic signals [36,40]. High alert-override rates [29] further suggest that a tool sitting one tier above clinical intuition will be treated as noise unless its justifications are transparent. The results are therefore consistent with the absence of a shared grading standard rather than with error in any one tool.""")

